}

^["risk_model"]{
 hard_failures: ["columns that disagree with the fingerprint's node count", "a reduction whose result depends on the order of rows in a band"]
 soft_failures: ["a 32-bit shape collision, caught by the column comparison before two shapes merge"]
 non_negotiables: ["the parse receipt rides with the columns, so a prose fallback cannot pass as structure", "no new hash function: words and shapes are prefixes of the one SHA-256"]
}

^["validation"]{
 hypotheses: ["every parse-whole surface lays out with rows equal to its fingerprint total, child spans inside their parents, siblings in source order, and the same bytes twice"]
 negative_controls: ["renaming words keeps a shape; respacing keeps a content; a changed connector changes a shape"]
 demo_path: ["spw form .agents/plans/inquiry-loop/wip.spw --columns", "spw form .agents/plans --rhymes --min 12 --max-files 400", "spw form .agents/plans/interpretation-trials --rhymes --sigil ?"]
}

^["stream"]{
 # Append entries chronologically. Never edit prior entries.
 # >>[YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM] <type> — <content>
 # Types: observe, decide, surprise, revise, blocked, taste, rebase, hygiene, implement, verify, note
 >>[2026-11-11 19:05] implement — spw.frame/1 lands in seed experimental: frameSource and frameAst give one row per node in preorder by source position (kind, mark, parent, depth, size, start, end, word, word span), the kind table, the parse receipt and bands deepest first; reduceUp runs a visit per band, frameForms reduces shape (kinds and marks) and content (words in) with the one SHA-256, and findRhymes reports the largest repeated shapes as templates with holes and fillers, or refrains. spw frame prints one file's bands, --json emits the columns, --rhymes reads paths, --sigil ? keeps wonders. First read over the 288 plan surfaces: the plan template's cache frame rhymes in 94 files, the commit ledger line 198 times, the tag lists 211 and 168 times; the trials file's wonder cards rhyme with each other.
 >>[2026-11-11 19:16] surprise — 400 sibling rows overlap across the plan surfaces, every one a Scope: in a ledger line .[name](hash): "…" the parser hangs the (hash) scope on the expression while the binding's span runs over it, so a node's span holds a node that is not under it. The frame keeps the parser's tree as it is; the hash also lexes as a number and a name, which is why the ledger template reads .[_](__): _.
 >>[2026-11-11 19:19] verify — Laws: 1,342 surfaces in this repo frame to 974,325 rows with no child span outside its parent, no node reached twice, no unknown kind, and the same bytes on a second frame of every 25th; rows equal to the fingerprint and siblings in source order hold by construction. Over the 289 plan surfaces, 22,412 identifier, particle and plain annotation words hash exactly from their spans; every refrain shares one content and no rhyme does; of 4,802 rhyme instances 4,672 give back their template from their own words ignoring spacing and comments, 109 differ only by a trailing comma, and 21 by the separator between a [ ] frame's items, which the tree does not keep. Review fixes before this entry: holes read from the word column, an annotation's hole takes its apposition, a close label no longer stretches a word span over a body, --sigil keeps shapes that open with the sigil and filters before covering, and templates leave comments out. 14 seed tests, 3 CLI tests, tsc clean, poll-review clean.
 >>[2026-11-11 19:23] revise — Two claims in the verify entry above are true by construction, as rows equal to the fingerprint are: once holes read the word column, every refrain shares one content and no rhyme does. The independent evidence is the rebuild law and the 22,412 words that hash from their spans. --sigil now keeps a shape only when the head is the operation or reaches it through pass-through rows (seed, sequence, expression) with one child each, so a block that begins with a wonder or brackets one is not a wonder; the trials file reads 7 wonder rhymes, not 8. The 94 cache-frame files are of the 288 plan surfaces read before this plan was added.
 >>[2026-11-11 20:53] implement — The parser keeps what the plates showed it dropping: a [ ] frame records its separators aligned to its gaps (separators[i] between content[i] and content[i+1], undefined where juxtaposed), as a sequence does, and a frame or a sequence keeps a separator written after its last item apart as trailing. sepByOptionalKept returns items with the separators after them, and sepByOptional delegates to it, so its parse events are unchanged. Additive: no node is added or moved, so no reading drifts; the seed suite passes (1,082 tests) with two new separator tests. In the plate, a frame's glyph is now its gaps (and trailing), so #[a ; b], #[a, b], #[a b] and #[a, b,] differ in shape.
 >>[2026-11-11 20:53] decide — Renamed before commit, at the owner's ask to align names: the probe is a plate (spw.plate/1, plateSource, spw plate), not a frame, since frame names the [ ] container (FrameNode, the Frame kind row, the frame-name rename); plate is an atlas plate, a whole subject laid flat on one page, and the printer's plate beside stencil, which carries form without content. The written-structure column is glyph, not mark, since mark names the # marks the editor renames and nest-path already calls a container's punctuation its glyph. The plan slug follows (plain-frame to plate-rhymes); entries above keep the words they were written in. The owner's question keeps its own words, a frame of plain data.
 >>[2026-11-11 21:05] decide — Plate felt like a bad name or a missed abstraction to the owner, and it was the second: plate (like frame before it) was a new noun for three things the IR spine already names. The owner chose no new noun. The layout is the parse tree in columns (parseColumns, spw.parse-columns/1), a dense form of the parse IR that the fingerprint and geometry bytecode can be read from as histograms; shape and content are the two finest grains of likeness (likeness.ts), above the skeleton nest-path reads and the census the fingerprint counts; rhymes are a form product, so the CLI is spw form --columns and --rhymes, walking files as form does. The plan slug follows (plate-rhymes to parse-columns), as do the journal and wonder_trial_columns; entries above keep the words they were written in.
 >>[2026-11-11 21:15] verify — Under the new names: likeness steps are rungs (a grain is what a step moves by, wonder_trial_grain), and counts say nodes (a row here is a table row, not a screen row). spw form --rhymes --max-files 400 reads all 289 plan surfaces: 639 rhymes and 12 refrains, the cache frame in 95 files (this plan's own among them), the ledger line 198 and 177 times, the tag lists 211, 168 and 113; the counts match the earlier build. A walk that stops at --max-files now says so. The lexer reads 2010b917 as NUMBER 2010 then IDENTIFIER b917, confirming the ledger split. Suites: seed 1,085, CLI 412, runtime 126, all passing; LSP 404 with the known clean-HEAD failure; tsc clean; geometry.ts back under 600 lines with the column modes in form-columns.ts.
 >>[2026-11-11 21:15] observe — What a parser change owes here: host-receipts (a receipt per host) and reading-drift (every line whose reading changes) are both at phase seed with no tool yet, so neither ran. The separator change adds token fields only (separators and trailing on frames, trailing on sequences); no node is added, moved or removed, and the walker that counts nodes skips tokens, so no line's tree changes; the seed, CLI, runtime and LSP suites agree. Frames built by the match-pattern grammar (grammar/patterns.ts) still carry no separators. spw form refuses a path outside its workspace, so rhymes across hosts wait on ?[across_hosts].
 >>[2026-11-12 09:19] observe — Follow-up to the 2026-11-11 21:15 entry, which said reading-drift had no tool: the gate exists, uncommitted on main 2026-11-12, as spw fingerprint --readings, and it is built on these columns (experimental/readings.ts reads kind, glyph, word, parent and previous sibling per node). Its control on the build before 179f4c29 shows 0 drifted lines over 304 surfaces here and 100 in the story consumer, read in place; part of that zero is by construction, since the gate leaves frame and sequence separators out as spelling. parseColumns takes a path, so a surface reads under the dialect its path selects. Evidence for wonder_trial_columns: the first second reader of the columns wanted one they do not carry, which slot a brace pair fills.
 >>[2026-11-13 17:21] implement — Shape match (seed experimental, spw.shape-match/1): every place a form is written, given by example with holes. _ stands for any one subtree, ... for any further siblings, and a _ alone inside a word (lens(_)) for any text there. Each match gives its span and what filled each hole. The example is read inside a body, so it parses as the same text does inside a card, and an example of several lines matches a run of neighbours. It runs on the columns: candidates come from one scan of the kind, glyph and word columns, and a hole skips a candidate subtree in one step, since a subtree is the range [i, i + size[i]).
 >>[2026-11-13 17:21] surprise — Three readings had to be found by running examples against the corpus. A _ in a frame is wrapped in an expression the written value does not have, so a holder chain ending in a hole is the hole. A sequence's glyph spells every separator, so an example ending in ... compares only the separators both sides have; {_, ...} matches a body of one part and not one written with semicolons. And the scope of a lens mark lives inside its word, lens(comparison), so holes inside a word were needed for the card header to match at all.
 >>[2026-11-13 17:21] implement — The columns as bytes (spw.parse-columns-bytes/1): a JSON header, then each column as the bytes it already is, every column on a four-byte boundary so it can be viewed in place. Over 1,312 tracked surfaces: parsing took 90.7 s; reading the same columns back from bytes 57 ms and from JSON 206 ms; 36.6 MB as bytes against 41.9 MB as JSON; all 1,312 read back exactly.
 >>[2026-11-13 17:21] implement — spw form --match reads columns from the nearest of three tiers: this process's memory, .spw/gen/session/columns, then the parser. The disk key is the text's hash under a fingerprint of the parser's own source (182 files, 14 ms to hash), so a parser change is a miss and never a stale tree; where that source cannot be read the disk tier is off and says why. --cache read never writes, --cache off parses everything. Over this repository the same example took 104.9 s cold and 3.1 s warm (566 ms reading columns), with the same 29 hits; the last line of every run says which tiers gave the columns and what they cost.
 >>[2026-11-13 17:21] verify — 9 tests on shape match (holes of each kind, separators, runs, word holes, outermost and nested; every line fixture matches itself with nothing to fill; a fixture with its first part replaced by _ matches with that part as the filler), 4 on the bytes, 7 on the cache and the match output. Four mutants each fail a test; a fifth found a rule that never ran (a one-part body has its own sequence node), which was removed. Seed 1,189 pass, CLI 448, LSP 405, runtime 137, DOM 3, parser release 25, tsc clean.
 >>[2026-11-13 17:21] note — Not built, and each could start from these: a match heard (each hit's span through pairSound or the spoken form); the fingerprint and geometry bytecode derived from the cached columns instead of a fresh parse, which would carry the cache's gain to census and graph; matches as edits (fill a hole with new text and write the line back through the line kit); a host reading spw.parse-columns-bytes/1 in a worker. The cache keys parse products only; runtime consequence (what a run does with a tree) has no key yet.
}

^["open"]{
 ?[columns]: ~"../interpretation-trials/interpretation-trials.spw#wonder_trial_columns"
 ?[naming]: ~"../interpretation-trials/interpretation-trials.spw#wonder_trial_rhyme"
 ?[shader_hash]: "Does a shader run the shape reduction as written (SHA-256 per row, all u32 operations), or take its own mix with the CPU column as the check?"
 ?[across_hosts]: "spw form refuses a path outside the workspace it runs in (Path is outside the consumer root), so rhymes across the mounted consumers need another way in: a read-only flag, running inside each consumer's own copy, or a list of roots the workspace declares. Which?"
 ?[parse_cost]: "Rhymes over the 288 plan surfaces took about two minutes under load, nearly all of it parsing; should spw form read a corpus memo of parses?"
}

